Zombie Process – Dead but still haunting your system.

Socho ek aadmi mar gaya... par uska naam abhi bhi office ke attendance me aa raha hai.
Bas wahi hota hai zombie process me.

What is a Zombie Process?
In Linux, when a child process finishes execution, it doesn’t vanish instantly.
It sends an exit status to the parent.
If the parent doesn’t read this (using wait()), that child turns into a Zombie — a dead process that still lives in the process table.

Body gone, soul stuck. Welcome to the undead army of your OS.


How to identify one?
         ps aux | grep 'Z'

Agar STAT column me Z hai — samajh jao, ek bhoot chhupa hua hai.

These zombies take up space in your process table. Too many? And boom — system crash.


Real-World Scenario:
You deployed a long-running backend service. It forks child processes for data handling.
You forgot wait() logic.
After 2 days?
     App slow ho gaya
     Node unresponsive
     Alert: “Too many processes”

You check — 300+ zombie processes.
They were dead. But still eating your resources.

Fix?
Add wait() / waitpid() in your parent process
Or gracefully kill the parent
Final nuclear option: Reboot (But never directly on prod 😅)


For Freshers:
Zombies are not just interview questions.
They are real, and they’ll mess your system if ignored.
Handle them like a boss — because real DevOps isn't about running commands, it's about knowing why you're running them.



Moral of the story?
Har process ka antim sanskar zaroori hai.
Na karo to wo system pe bhoot ban ke chhaya rahega.
